   P. Y. and Do, A. T.},
Book-Group-Author = {IEEE},
Title = {Performance Evaluation of a RISC-V CPU at Cryogenic Temperature for
   Future Quantum Control},
Booktitle = {2024 21ST INTERNATIONAL SOC DESIGN CONFERENCE, ISOCC},
Series = {International SoC Design Conference},
Year = {2024},
Pages = {67-68},
Note = {21st International SoC Design Conference-ISOCC, Sapporo, JAPAN, AUG
   19-22, 2024},
Abstract = {Cryogenic CMOS is garnering lots of attentions as promising solutions
   for future large scale quantum computers. However, most of recent works
   focus on improving the analog front-end to enhance qubit drive and
   read-out fidelity, leveraging on simple customized digital control. We
   anticipate that future practical quantum control system-on-chip (SoC)
   will require full-fledged general purpose processors operating at
   cryogenic temperature to enable flexibility and programmability when
   running quantum applications. To advance this vision, this work reports
   a 32-bit RISC-V central processing unit fabricated using a ultra-low
   threshold voltage 28nm CMOS process. Our tests showed that even with
   standard digital implementation flow, the chip is fully functional at
   temperature of 77K with similar power per MHz as compared to at room
   temperature (i.e. 300K), while consuming similar to 300x less leakage
   power. This paves an important ground work towards a RISC-V based SoC
   for large scale quantum computing applications at 4K temperature.},
